0.25 w BT/F4 10 Tf0 Tc 0 Tw 316.901 131.75 TD [ (1-)1 (1)] TJ/F7 24 Tf-172.776 491.25 TD (1. Introduction)Tj/F7 14 Tf0 -45.25 TD (1.1. A Brief Introduction to Diversity Database)Tj/F5 10 Tf18 -21.5 TD (Diversity Database)Tj/F6 10 Tf92.531 0 TD -0.165 Tw (\252 is a powerful, \337exible software package for analyzing )Tj-92.531 -12 TD -0.169 Tw (and comparing biological samples across multiple 1-D electrophoresis gels. It )Tj0 -12 TD 0 Tw (combines image acquisition and analysis features with databasing functions )TjT* -0.017 Tc (for creating and managing large catalogs of protein and DNA samples.)Tj0 -20 TD 0 Tc (Using Diversity Database\325s band identi\336cation tools and powerful search )Tj0 -12 TD (engine, you can quickly determine similarities among thousands of different )TjT* -0.018 Tc (biological samples in hundreds of gel images. You can classify your samples )TjT* 0 Tc -0.442 Tw (by protein type, enzyme, DNA probe, or any other user-de\336ned category, and )TjT* -0.018 Tc 0 Tw (perform similiarity searches within single or multiple categories. You can )TjT* 0 Tc (display your results in variety of statistical, graphical, and image reports.)Tj0 -20 TD -0.015 Tc (The software runs in a Windows or Macintosh environment and has an easy-)Tj0 -12 TD 0 Tc (to-use interface with standard pull-down menus, toolbars, and keyboard )TjT* (commands.)Tj0 -20 TD (Diversity Database can analyze a wide variety of biological data, including )Tj0 -12 TD (radioactive, chemiluminescent, \337uorescent, and color-stained samples )TjT* -0.354 Tw (acquired from densitometers, phosphor imagers, \337uorescent imagers, and gel )TjT* 0 Tw (documentation systems.)Tj0 -20 TD -0.027 Tc (You can share your  images among all the )Tj/F5 10 Tf186.262 0 TD 0 Tc (Discovery Series\252)Tj/F6 10 Tf91.279 0 TD ( software, or )Tj-277.542 -12 TD (convert them into TIFF format for compatibility with other Macintosh and )Tj0 -12 TD (Windows applications.)Tj/F7 14 Tf-18 -38.5 TD (1.2. How Diversity Database Databases Gel Images)Tj/F6 10 Tf18 -21.5 TD -0.016 Tc (Every Diversity Database database starts with a single gel image. You begin )Tj0 -12 TD 0 Tc (building the database by identifying and matching the unique bands in that )TjT* (gel\321these matched bands are called band types.)TjETendstreamendobj54 0 obj2331endobj52 0 obj<</Type /Page/Parent 38 0 R/Resources <</Font <</F4 16 0 R /F5 18 0 R /F6 20 0 R /F7 25 0 R >>/ProcSet 2 0 R>>/Contents 53 0 R>>endobj56 0 obj<</Length 57 0 R>>stream
0.25 w BT/F4 12 Tf0 Tc 0 Tw 108.125 662.5 TD (Diversity Database User Guide)Tj/F4 10 Tf172.776 -530.75 TD [ (1-)1 (2)] TJ/F6 10 Tf-154.776 500.5 TD (Band types are used to link samples across gels. They are de\336ned by their )Tj0 -12 TD (position and \(if standards have been run\) molecular weight or isoelectric )TjT* (point.)Tj0 -20 TD (As you add gels to the database, you add to your collection of band types. )Tj0 -12 TD -0.171 Tw (Every band in every gel in the database will be identi\336ed as a particular band )TjT* 0 Tw (type.)Tj0 -20 TD (Band types are grouped together into band sets; a band set includes all the )Tj0 -12 TD -0.021 Tc (band types that were created using the same enzyme, probe, etc. A database )TjT* -0.013 Tc (can contain multiple band sets; however, you can only perform searches and )TjT* 0 Tc (comparisons within a single band set.)Tj0 -20 TD -0.016 Tc (You can build your database to include hundreds of images and band types. )Tj0 -12 TD 0 Tc (The powerful search engine within Diversity Database can perform complex )TjT* (searches and comparisons on hundreds of samples within minutes.)Tj/F7 14 Tf-18 -38.5 TD (1.3. Digital Data and Signal Intensity)Tj/F6 10 Tf18 -21.5 TD (The Bio-Rad imaging devices supported by Diversity Database are light )Tj0 -12 TD (and/or radiation detectors that convert signals from biological samples into )TjT* (digital data. Diversity Database then displays the digital data on your )TjT* (computer screen, in the form of gray scale or color images.)Tj0 -20 TD (A data object as displayed on the computer is composed of tiny individual )Tj0 -12 TD -0.108 Tw (screen pixels. Each pixel has an X and Y coordinate, and a value Z. The X and )TjT* 0 Tw (Y coordinates are the pixel\325s horizontal and vertical positions on the image, )TjT* (and the Z value is the signal intensity of the pixel.)TjETendstreamendobj57 0 obj1896endobj55 0 obj<</Type /Page/Parent 38 0 R/Resources <</Font <</F4 16 0 R /F6 20 0 R /F7 25 0 R >>/ProcSet 2 0 R>>/Contents 56 0 R>>endobj61 0 obj<</Length 62 0 R>>stream
0.25 w BT/F4 12 Tf0 Tc 0 Tw 436.126 662.5 TD (Introduction)Tj/F4 10 Tf-119.224 -530.75 TD [ (1-)1 (3)] TJ/F10 9 Tf-140.806 339.25 TD (Fig.1-1. Representation of the pixels in two digitally imaged bands in a gel.)Tj/F6 10 Tf-13.97 -21.75 TD (For a data object to be visible and quanti\336able, the intensity of its clustered )Tj0 -12 TD (pixels must be higher than the intensity of the pixels that make up the )TjT* (background of the image. The total intensity of a data object is the sum of the )TjT* (intensities of all the pixels that make up the object. The mean intensity of a )TjT* (data object is the total intensity divided by the number of pixels in the object.)Tj0 -20 TD (The units of signal intensity are Optical Density \(O.D.\) in the case of the GS-)Tj0 -12 TD (700 and 710 densitometers, the Gel Doc with a white light source, or the )TjT* (Fluor-S and Fluor-S MAX MultiImagers with white light illumination. Signal )TjT* (intensity is expressed in counts when using the Personal FX or FX, or in the )TjT* (case of the Gel Doc, Fluor-S, or Fluor-S MAX when using the UV light source.)Tj/F7 14 Tf-18 -38.5 TD [ (1.4. About Gel )-4 (Quality)] TJ/F6 10 Tf18 -21.5 TD (Diversity Database is very tolerant of an assortment of electrophoretic )Tj0 -12 TD (artifacts. Lanes need not be perfectly straight or parallel. Bands need not be )TjT* (perfectly resolved.)Tj0 -20 TD (However, for accurate lane-based quantitation, we suggest that bands be )Tj0 -12 TD (reasonably \337at and horizontal. Lane-based quantitation involves calculating )TjT* -0.156 Tw (the average intensity of pixels across the band width and integrating over the )TjT* -0.017 Tc 0 Tw (band height. For the automatic band \336nder to function optimally, bands )TjT* 0 Tc (should be well-resolved.)TjET2 j 367.625 578.375 m 355.125 565.875 l 378.375 565.875 l 390.875 578.375 l 367.625 578.375 l s 0 j 1 g 367.5 576 1.75 14 ref 367.625 575.875 1.75 14 res 369.75 574.5 1 8.5 re