BT/F4 10 Tf0 Tc 0 Tw 316.901 131.75 TD [ (3-)1 (1)] TJ/F7 24 Tf-172.776 491.25 TD [ (3. T)37 (utorials)] TJ/F6 10 Tf18 -30.75 TD (This chapter provides four tutorials that are designed to guide you through )Tj0 -12 TD (the main features and functions of Diversity Database. All of the features )TjT* (described in this chapter are discussed in greater detail in subsequent )TjT* (chapters.)Tj0 -20 TD (These lessons utilize three sample databases and six images that were )Tj0 -12 TD (installed when you installed Diversity Database. \(See below for the location )TjT* (of these \336les.\))Tj0 -20 TD -0.033 Tc (The lessons are designed to be read sequentially. However, the \336les created )Tj0 -12 TD -0.02 Tc (and altered in each lesson are independent of those in the other lessons. You )TjT* 0 Tc (do not have to perform the steps in Lesson 1 before you can move on to )TjT* (Lesson 2, and so forth.)Tj0 -20 TD (Lesson 1 describes how to create a new database, add a gel image to it, and )Tj0 -12 TD (de\336ne lanes and identify bands in that gel.)Tj0 -20 TD (Lesson 2 describes how to create standards and a band set and use the band )Tj0 -12 TD (set to match the bands in your image.)Tj0 -20 TD (Lesson 3 describes how to create a band set without standards and use it to )Tj0 -12 TD (match the bands in your image.)Tj0 -20 TD (Lesson 4 describes how to add a gel to a preexisting database and outlines )Tj0 -12 TD (some of the similarity searches you can perform on the images in your )TjT* (database.)Tj/F7 11 Tf-9 -27.5 TD (Location of Sample Images and Databases)Tj/F6 10 Tf9 -20.5 TD (If you are using Windows, the sample databases and images are installed in )Tj0 -12 TD (the Div Database folder in the Sample Images folder in the Discovery Series )TjT* -0.392 Tw (directory on your hard drive \(Program Files > Bio-Rad > The Discovery Series )TjT* 0 Tw (> Sample Images > Div Database\).)Tj0 -20 TD (If you are using a Macintosh, the sample databases and images are installed )Tj0 -12 TD (in the Sample Images folder inside the Diversity Database folder on your )TjT* (hard disk.)TjETendstreamendobj311 0 obj2135endobj308 0 obj<</Type /Page/Parent 309 0 R/Resources <</Font <</F4 16 0 R /F6 20 0 R /F7 25 0 R >>/ProcSet 2 0 R>>/Contents 310 0 R>>endobj313 0 obj<</Length 314 0 R>>stream
BT/F4 12 Tf0 Tc 0 Tw 108.125 662.5 TD (Diversity Database User Guide)Tj/F4 10 Tf172.776 -530.75 TD [ (3-)1 (2)] TJ/F7 14 Tf-172.776 498 TD (3.1. Lesson 1. Creating a Database and Preparing an Image)Tj/F6 10 Tf18 -21.5 TD (In this lesson, you will create a new database, add a gel image to that )Tj0 -12 TD (database, and de\336ne the lanes and bands in the gel image. \(See Chapters 5\3207 )TjT* (for more detailed explanations of the commands described in this lesson.\))Tj/F7 12 Tf-18 -24.25 TD (3.1.a. Creating a New Database)Tj/F6 10 Tf18 -20.75 TD (Every database starts with a good, clean gel image with well-de\336ned bands. )Tj0 -12 TD -0.016 Tc (You should choose a gel image that has most of the bands in which you are )TjT* 0 Tc (interested in your research. The more distinct bands in your \322starter\323 image, )TjT* -0.141 Tw (the less work you will have to do when you add other images to the database.)Tj0 -20 TD 0 Tw (In this example, we will create a new database using Image.4 as the \322starter\323 )Tj0 -12 TD (gel image.)Tj0 -20 TD (1.)Tj18 0 TD (From the main toolbar, click on Open. In the Open dialog box, select )Tj0 -12 TD -0.202 Tw (Image.4 from the Sample Images folder. The raw image will be displayed.)Tj/F10 9 Tf108.745 -173 TD 0 Tw (Fig. 3-1. Image.4.)Tj/F6 10 Tf-126.745 -21.5 TD (2.)Tj18 0 TD (From the File menu, select New Database. This will open the Database )Tj0 -12 TD (Summary form.)TjETq 261 0 0 133.5 157.5 281.5 cm/I28 Doendstreamendobj314 0 obj1500endobj316 0 obj<</Type /XObject/Subtype /Image/Name /I28/Filter /FlateDecode /Width 522/Height 267/BitsPerComponent 8/ColorSpace [ /Indexed /DeviceRGB 255 315 0 R ]/Length 317 0 R>>stream
